# Designing for the Web That Already Exists

Web design often begins with the question, "What can we invent?"

I think another useful question is, "What does the web already do well?"

Browsers come with decades of conventions: links look and behave like links, headings create structure, forms have familiar controls, and URLs can point directly to information.

Those conventions are not obstacles to design. They are materials we can design with.

## Familiarity Has Value

A familiar interaction requires less explanation.

People already know that underlined text is probably a link. They understand that a browser's Back button should take them somewhere meaningful. They expect text to be selectable and pages to respond to zoom.

When a design preserves these expectations, the interface becomes easier to understand before the user has learned anything about the site.

## Accessibility Benefits Too

Semantic HTML gives browsers and assistive technologies useful information about a document.

A heading should be a heading because it describes structure, not merely because it needs larger text.

A navigation region should be navigation because that meaning helps people move through the page.

Many accessibility improvements begin with choosing the correct native element and then resisting the urge to replace its behavior unnecessarily.

## Progressive Enhancement

I prefer to begin with a page that works as a document.

From there, enhancements can be layered on top.

If an interaction benefits from JavaScript, add it. If a browser feature can improve presentation, use it. But the underlying content should remain available when those enhancements are unavailable.

This approach also tends to produce simpler systems, something I explored in [Building Smaller Websites](::building-smaller-websites).

## Design With the Platform

The browser is not an empty canvas.

It already knows how to lay out text, follow links, navigate history, submit forms, display media, and adapt to user preferences.

Good web design can take advantage of those capabilities rather than rebuilding them from scratch.

Sometimes the most durable design decision is simply to let the web behave like the web.